WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:11.139
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Let us move on now to the
next agenda item.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:16.300
&gt;&gt;Richard Barker: Thank you, Chairman. I think
the report is fairly self-explanatory, but

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:23.640
I would just like to alight on the aspect
of winter resilience for the NHS. Board members

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:27.020
will recall that, last time, both the Chief
Executive and myself remarked on the performance

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:32.460
over the Christmas period, and the fact that
the NHS had stood up quite well over that

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:38.929
period. We now have the statistics to prove
it. This report includes the December analysis.

00:00:38.929 --> 00:00:42.469
Performance on the A&amp;E four-hour wait was
still not where we would like it to be, but

00:00:42.469 --> 00:00:46.889
nevertheless at 91% was significantly better
than the same period last year. In December

00:00:46.889 --> 00:00:53.829
2014, that equivalent figure was 89.9%. We
have seen better performance in ambulance

00:00:53.829 --> 00:00:55.979
response times as well.

00:00:55.979 --> 00:00:59.999
One statistic which is not in the report,
but I thought colleagues would be interested

00:00:59.999 --> 00:01:05.820
in, is that in the previous winter - December
2014 - we had a lot of problems with excess

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.760
trolley waits, 12-hour trolley waits.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:13.920
We actually had 186 of those in December 2014
alone. The equivalent figure for December

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.480
2015 is still regrettable but was 29, so a
significant reduction. I think that does bear

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.670
out the fact that, this winter, the NHS has
proved more resilient. It has coped better.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:28.860
We have had much fewer extremes of serious
operational issues and performance, and I

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:33.560
can say that that actually prevails up until
this point. Clearly, again, we will see next

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:37.630
month the performance figures for January.
So overall performance is not where we want

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:42.070
it to be, but it is certainly in a better
position than it was this time last year.

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:45.730
I think I will just make the final point that,
as I said in the previous item, we are in

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:49.170
the middle of the operational planning round.
We are working very closely with our colleagues

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:53.680
in NHS Improvement, and we are making sure
that every local system has a performance

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:58.450
trajectory around elective and emergency targets,
mental health targets and cancer targets for

00:01:58.450 --> 00:01:59.170
the next year.

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:02.530
Where they are currently behind those targets,
they have been asked to deliver recovery plans

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:05.460
and demonstrate through next year's plans
how they are going to get back on course.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:09.599
So that is a key focus of our joint endeavour
at this time.

00:02:09.599 --> 00:02:12.769
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Any questions? Moira.

00:02:12.769 --> 00:02:21.469
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Chair, can I ask Richard about
the transfers of care? What is his sense of

00:02:21.469 --> 00:02:27.019
that significant increase? Also, does acute
care mean 'not social care'? Because the implication

00:02:27.019 --> 00:02:33.139
is that that has gone up as well, but proportionately,
as it were.

00:02:33.139 --> 00:02:39.349
&gt;&gt;Richard Barker: The DTOC area is a challenge
and a difficulty in a number of ways. One

00:02:39.349 --> 00:02:43.859
thing that I can report is that we have set
up a cross-Government initiative to work with

00:02:43.859 --> 00:02:48.739
social care agencies and NHS to make sure
we really improve our position on this.

00:02:48.739 --> 00:02:53.980
The reported position is currently that around
3.5% of our beds are blocked at any one time,

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:59.040
which is a lot in terms of what it means in
terms of the ability to smoothly discharge

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:05.650
patients, but it is still 3.5%. In other words,
96.5% of beds are not blocked in that way.

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:10.480
I think one of the concerns that I have personally
- and it is a personal concern; I have not

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:14.900
got the evidence for it yet - is that there
are a lot of patients who are in beds who,

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:18.849
similarly, could be discharged more quickly
who are not yet classified as a DTOC.

00:03:18.849 --> 00:03:24.060
This is because, to be classified as a delayed
transfer of care, you need to have a multidisciplinary

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:26.919
assessment both by the NHS professional and
the social care professional. So you have

00:03:26.919 --> 00:03:30.290
decided this patient needs to be discharged,
but the delay is that it actually has not

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:34.370
happened yet. There are many patients who
are deemed medically fit but who have not

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:36.219
been through that process.

00:03:36.219 --> 00:03:39.980
One of the things that we will be trying to
do, working hard with providers and social

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:45.069
care, is to do those assessments earlier and
more quickly and so make sure they are ready

00:03:45.069 --> 00:03:47.739
for discharge, but the negative consequence
of that is that we might actually increase

00:03:47.739 --> 00:03:50.859
the number of people who are within that definition.

00:03:50.859 --> 00:03:54.799
So it may look superficially like we are getting
worse when actually we are improving, because

00:03:54.799 --> 00:03:59.279
we are identifying those people earlier and
moving them through. I think we need to work

00:03:59.279 --> 00:04:03.370
with the cross-Government initiative and forecast
what that impact is going to be.

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:07.169
As I said, there is a slight danger that,
superficially, things might look poorer in

00:04:07.169 --> 00:04:11.189
a short time, but it would be because we are
actually intervening earlier and making sure

00:04:11.189 --> 00:04:15.340
these discharge packages are ready, and then
making sure they are actually executed.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:24.250
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Okay. Any other questions?
Richard, there are a number of these. Whilst

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:31.290
the overall position I think shows improvement,
which is great - thanks, as always, to all

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:36.820
colleagues across the NHS for their efforts
in this - there are still a number where either

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:43.230
we are not hitting the target or we are only
just hitting the target, RTT probably the

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:46.440
most visible in some respects.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:52.510
I guess the Board should have some assurance
on what is being done to try and improve our

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:53.410
performance there.

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:56.820
&gt;&gt;Richard Barker: Yes, indeed. Well, as I
said, the main assurance is through the planning

00:04:56.820 --> 00:05:02.350
process to make sure that every individual
community is working really hard on these

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:03.220
targets.

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:06.590
Much clearer this year, I think, than they
have been in the past about the demand and

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:10.480
capacity relationship. I think some of the
planning has been not as strong as it should

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:11.260
have been.

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:16.100
People have identified 12 months' worth of
activity which they know needs to be delivered,

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:19.780
but they have not really thought, month on
month, week on week, how that will be delivered.

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:25.340
When the system gets under pressure, that
activity is not delivered in the planned way,

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.670
really making sure that is carefully thought
through.

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:33.720
At the same time, we are trying to support
local systems through national initiatives,

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:39.630
looking at things like the many procedures
that perhaps are of limited clinical effectiveness.

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:42.790
There is good evidence of that, so we need
to make sure that people are aware of that

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:47.190
- that both referring clinicians are aware
of it and receiving clinicians are able to

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:49.300
look at their treatment options.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:54.770
There are also many better alternatives than
some of the traditional referrals into hospital.

00:05:54.770 --> 00:05:58.330
Just an example: with many of the musculoskeletal
conditions that people suffer from, direct

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:03.250
access to good physiotherapy could be a good
alternative and take demand off the hospital

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:04.430
part of the system.

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:08.690
So we are working through a programme that
is called Right Care, which is an evidence-based

00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:13.970
programme that supports CCG commissioners
to make those decisions. So it is a mixture

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:20.040
of local operational work to improve things,
but also some national support to help people

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:21.240
to actually plan the best way.

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:26.690
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: I have been fortunate to
spend some time with the Right Care team,

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:28.870
and also to see some of the things in action.

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:35.770
I have great hope for that, particularly as
an awful lot of it does not involve any investment

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:41.310
and does not involve any change in the sense
of working practices, but it is absolutely

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:47.490
things that can have a dramatic impact on
patients: on reducing variability, on greater

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:49.740
consistency and on greater quality.

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:54.600
Therefore, I know we are rolling that out
as fast as we can. There is a skill issue

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:58.740
and everything, but there are ways in which
we can make a real difference there. Okay.

00:06:58.740 --> 00:06:59.650
Thank you.

